        <td class="NewsText" style="font-size: 15px"><a href="liya">Liya</a> Linux is an Arch Linux-based, rolling-release distribution. The project's latest snapshot, version 2026.08.29, introduces a new versioning scheme, removal of AI content and tools, and a switch from X.Org to XLibre. "<span class="Quote">AI-generated content and tools have been removed from the default Liya Linux experience. While AI can be useful in some situations, a number of users found the previous implementation difficult to use, and it also didn't really fit with the general philosophy and preferences of the Linux community. Because of that, we've decided to move away from it rather than keep something around simply for the sake of having it. Modularisation of tweaks and configurations - the way Liya's tweaks and configuration files are handled has been changed. Previously, many of these tweaks were statically built into the system. They are now handled through pacman, which means they can be updated independently instead of requiring changes to the entire system. The same approach is being used for configuration files as well. This should make future updates easier to maintain and allow individual components to be changed without rebuilding everything. A big thanks to CachyOS for their tweaks and the work that helped inspire some of these changes. XLibre instead of X11 - Liya Linux now uses XLibre instead of the traditional X11/X.Org stack.</span>" Additional information can be found in the <a href="https://liyalinux.freeflarum.com/d/159-releng-20260829-gauri">release announcement</a>. <a href="https://liyalinux.sdhsm128.workers.dev/#download">Download</a>: <a href="https://downloads.sourceforge.net/liya-2025/Liya-2026.08.29-x86_64-v2.iso">Liya-2026.08.29-x86_64-v2.iso</a> (4,465MB, <a href="https://sourceforge.net/projects/liya-2025/files/Gauri/">MD5</a>, <a href="table.php?distribution=liya&amp;pkglist=true&amp;version=2026.08.29#pkglist">pkglist</a>).</td>
